# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Compass — local defaults. Copy to .env; no secret is required for cold start.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Pooled application connection (transaction-mode pooler in production).
DATABASE_URL=postgresql://compass:compass@localhost:5432/compass

# Direct session connection. Migrations, advisory locks and pg-boss maintenance
# MUST use this one: role/session DDL cannot run through a transaction pooler.
DATABASE_URL_DIRECT=postgresql://compass:compass@localhost:5432/compass

# IANA timezone used when a team has no working calendar configured yet. The report
# is dated in this zone, and the window it covers is that zone's previous civil day.
COMPASS_DEFAULT_TIMEZONE=Europe/London

# The team `/` opens on. The seeded organization declares `platform` and `checkout`
# (both scrum) and `insights` (kanban); see seed/MANIFEST.md.
COMPASS_DEFAULT_TEAM=platform

# When the worker generates each team's daily report, as `HH:MM` in *that team's* timezone.
#
# Defaults to 06:00, which is early enough to precede the 07:30 subscription send time so a
# delivery never finds a missing report. Each team's own timezone comes from the roster, so
# this is the local wall-clock time and DST is handled for you. Unset or malformed, 06:00 is
# used. See docs/DEVELOPMENT.md § The scheduled pipeline.
# COMPASS_GENERATION_LOCAL_TIME=06:00

# Narration is optional. Absent, reports render through the deterministic
# template renderer and say so in the page footer — complete, correct, templated.
# Present, Claude writes the six sections from the structured payload only, and
# every number, date, identifier and name it writes is checked against that payload
# before publication. See docs/DEVELOPMENT.md § Narration and grounding.
# ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=

# The narration model. Defaults to claude-sonnet-5 (docs/ARCHITECTURE.md).
# COMPASS_NARRATION_MODEL=claude-sonnet-5

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The first owner.
#
# Seats are invite-only and only an owner can grant the owner role, so an organization
# with no owner has no path to its first one. The boot script creates this seat on every
# start; it is idempotent and never overwrites a password you have since changed.
#
# Unset, the published demonstration credentials below are used, so `docker compose up`
# gives you an account to sign in with. `/api/health` reports `seats: not_configured` and
# `/account` prints them for as long as that is true — a deployment on a published
# password must not be able to look healthy. Set both before this holds real data.
# See docs/ARCHITECTURE.md § Sessions, tokens and the four-role matrix.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# COMPASS_OWNER_EMAIL=you@your-company.com
# COMPASS_OWNER_PASSWORD=at-least-twelve-characters
# COMPASS_OWNER_NAME=Your Name

# The absolute origin Compass puts in a mailed sign-in, reset or invitation link.
#
# Required for any deployment not reached over loopback. It is deliberately NOT taken from
# the request: POST /api/auth/password-reset is public and answers the same for an address
# with a seat and one without, so a caller who could choose the host would choose where
# somebody else's single-use sign-in token pointed. Unset and off localhost, Compass
# refuses to send rather than guess, and says so with a 503 naming this variable.
#
# Unset on localhost — a laptop, or `docker compose up` opened on localhost:3000 — links
# point at that origin, which is what makes the zero-config path work with nothing set.
# COMPASS_BASE_URL=https://compass.your-company.com

# Set when TLS terminates at a proxy that does not send `x-forwarded-proto`. Without it
# the session cookie is issued without `Secure`, and with it over plain HTTP the browser
# would drop the cookie silently. Only one of those is a state you can debug.
# COMPASS_ASSUME_HTTPS=true

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Delivery: email and Slack.
#
# Both channels are optional and the product is fully usable without either — the
# report is always readable at `/`. With no key configured, a scheduled delivery is
# recorded in `delivery_log` with status `skipped` and a sentence naming the missing
# variable, the worker carries on, and `/api/health` reports the capability as
# not_configured. Nothing is silently dropped and nothing crashes.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Resend API key. Also the transport for sign-in links, password resets and
# invitations: until it is set those are written to the process log instead.
# RESEND_API_KEY=

# The verified sending address emails come from, e.g. "Compass <daily@your-company.com>".
# Required alongside RESEND_API_KEY — Resend will not send from an unverified domain.
#
# Configure SPF, DKIM and DMARC on that domain in the Resend dashboard before sending
# to real inboxes; a daily that lands in spam is a daily nobody reads. Compass takes
# this address from configuration and never from a request, so a caller cannot choose
# what a report appears to be sent from.
# COMPASS_EMAIL_FROM=

# Slack bot token (`xoxb-…`), with `chat:write` scope. The same token delivers to a DM
# and to a channel: a subscription's target is a user id (`U…`) for a DM or `#channel`.
# SLACK_BOT_TOKEN=

# Slack signing secret, for verifying the feedback-button callbacks Slack posts back.
#
# Required for Slack feedback buttons to work at all: with this unset, `/api/slack/actions`
# refuses every interaction with a 503 rather than trusting an unverified request. Point
# Slack's interactivity request URL at `<COMPASS_BASE_URL>/api/slack/actions`.
#
# Verification is over the *raw* request body inside a 5-minute timestamp window. A valid
# signature proves the workspace, not the person, so a click is additionally matched from the
# acting Slack user to a Compass seat via `subscriptions.slack_user_id` — an unmapped or
# unauthorized click gets an ephemeral "you don't have access" and changes nothing.
# SLACK_SIGNING_SECRET=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Feedback links in the daily email.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# The HMAC secret the email's one-click feedback links are signed under. Any long random
# string; `openssl rand -base64 48` is fine.
#
# Optional, and its absence is a *supported* state rather than a misconfiguration: the email
# then carries the whole report with no action links, and the report page's own buttons still
# work. Compass will not fall back to a default key — an HMAC under a known secret is not a
# signature, and the link would let anybody dismiss anybody's risk.
#
# Each link is scoped to one item and one action, expires after 30 days, is single-use for
# anything that changes state, and never establishes a session.
# COMPASS_FEEDBACK_LINK_SECRET=
